A||||6||ThatRickGuy||88 rear brakes||||1||
Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||05-23-2001||11:17 AM||||Okay, after searchng the archives, I know that the pre '88s it is suggested to just do the C-clam/socket trick, my question is w/ the '88s, can you still do the C-clamp trick or do you need to turn the piston to get it back in? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||05-23-2001||11:38 AM||||GM specifies you rotate 88 pistons. They even have a tool for doing it w/o removing the caliper. (J 36621 acording to Haynes...) The regular tools will work, you'll just have to pull the caliper to use them.<P>I don't know if you can use the older method. I'd do it how GM says... <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||05-23-2001||11:43 AM||||I made a tool for rotating the piston out of an old brake pad. Just welded a piece of band iron to the old pad and sawed off the ears of the pad just past the pins. Works fine ||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||05-23-2001||11:46 AM||||Thanks for the help, at $100+, I really don't want to mess these up!||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||05-23-2001||11:50 AM|||| If you use a C-Clamp you will ruin them. I purchased one of those tools that clip on a ratchet for $10 and rotated the piston in.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChadMan||05-23-2001||12:41 PM||||They MUST be rotated.  If you're a cheapskate like me and don't want to buy the tool, you can accomplish this by putting a small centerpunch in one of the recesses on the piston and GENTLY tapping with a small hammer.  Tap easy - the pistons are a very soft alloy...<P>(Unfortunately it's been a year since I did this and I can't remember whether both pistons rotate clockwise to seat them, or not...)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||05-23-2001||01:35 PM||||The pistons should rotate opposite directions. (Each cable pulls from the front, correct?)||||reg||1
